# About P1 Compute Resources

The Pioneer Centre for Artificial Intelligence (P1) is a Danish cross-university AI research
centre and consortium. This site shows P1 affiliates how to access and use the high-performance
computing (HPC) facilities open to them.

<NeedsCallout />

<img src="/assets/PCAI_HQ.avif" alt="Pioneer Centre for AI headquarters" class="my-6 w-full rounded-lg" loading="lazy" />

P1 funds and helps run dedicated partitions at partner HPC facilities for researchers across its
partner universities. The compute coordinator holds admin on the systems and works with each
provider's HPC team.

More at the [P1 homepage](https://www.aicentre.dk/) and
[P1 Computing](https://www.aicentre.dk/computing).

## Available Compute Resources

P1 prioritizes smaller projects, which make up most research needs. Larger projects can move up
the [tiered compute resources](#hierarchy-of-compute-resources) to Tier 2 and Tier 1 facilities.
Training a large language or vision model from scratch may not fit our Tier 3 facilities, but it
can fit Gefion, a Tier 2 national facility built for large-scale model training. Very large
projects may be better served by their own funding.

## Support

P1 has no official support staff; the community helps each other. For the cluster, write to:

- Technical issues, such as broken or missing packages: [support@hpc.dtu.dk](mailto:support@hpc.dtu.dk)
- Policy issues, such as priority access or queueing: [compute-governance-p1@aicentre.dk](mailto:compute-governance-p1@aicentre.dk)

On the P1 Slack, [#compute](https://pioneercentreforai.slack.com/channels/compute) has
announcements and [#ask-compute](https://pioneercentreforai.slack.com/channels/ask-compute) takes
compute questions, including about the P1 partitions.

## HPC Governance Board

One representative per partner university. The board sets the policies for the P1 compute
resources and holds the mandate over them. Contact yours if your cluster signup has been pending
for more than a week, or for governance questions about your institution.

<People people={board} />

## Investing Your Compute Budget

P1 can help you buy hardware that becomes part of a P1 partition. You get priority access to it,
and other P1 researchers can use it when it is idle. Most HPC hardware lasts about 5 years.

An example:

> A researcher has 400k DKK for non-GDPR computing. P1 buys a 2xA100 node with priority access
> for the researcher. Of the last 100k DKK, we recommend some on personal computers with powerful
> GPUs and a large share held back for storage or cloud compute. Cloud budget covers a submission
> deadline when the shared clusters are too busy.

<ComputeBudget />

## Hierarchy of Compute Resources

Each tier suits a different scale of work.

<!-- md:replace tier-table -->
<ComputeTiers />
<!-- md:end -->

The P1 DTU and NGC HPCs are Tier 3. Gefion is Tier 2 and can support some Tier 1 workloads, but
the P1 sandbox on it is Tier 3 capacity, for maturing a workload on the hardware it will later run
on. Tier 2 scale on Gefion needs its own funding.

## Reading these docs with an AI agent

Give this site to a coding agent or chat assistant instead of pasting pages by hand:

- `https://hpc.aicentre.dk/llms.txt` lists every page with a one-line summary, per the
  [llms.txt convention](https://llmstxt.org/), so an agent fetches only what it needs.
- `https://hpc.aicentre.dk/llms-full.txt` is the whole site as one markdown file.
- Any page path plus `.md` gives that page as plain markdown, such as
  `https://hpc.aicentre.dk/providers/dtu.md`. The **View as Markdown** link in the page outline
  does the same.

These are built from the same source as the pages. They are guidance, not live system state: queue
limits and hardware change, and the provider documentation linked on each cluster page is the
authority.

